# Trivy CRITICAL/HIGH exceptions. Every surviving entry below is backed by evidence recorded in
# docs/image-footprint.md (IMG-04 disposition table) — per-image presence/absence, installed
# version vs. the CVE's own documented fix threshold, and (where relevant) exactly why the
# runtime never reaches the vulnerable code path. Before writing a new entry here, or before
# trusting an existing one, verify it the same way: re-check the image and the upstream advisory
# directly rather than assuming a runtime-exposure or fix-availability claim still holds (see
# docs/cve-handling.md).

# http-cache-semantics: max-stale handling in a SHARED cache can return another user's
# security-zeroed cached response, e.g. a Set-Cookie (CVE-2026-93748, CWE-524, GHSA-ch52-4w7c-c8xp,
# high, github_reviewed_at 2026-10-02, vulnerable range <= 4.2.0, NO first_patched_version, not
# withdrawn — re-read from the advisory API on 2026-10-08).
# Present in exactly ONE location, the API image only:
# /root/.cache/node/corepack/v1/pnpm/10.34.6/dist/node_modules/http-cache-semantics -> 4.2.0.
# Absent from the web image (`find / -path "*/node_modules/http-cache-semantics/package.json"`
# -> no hits; web has no corepack/pnpm cache at all) and absent from pnpm-lock.yaml (zero
# occurrences), so no application copy exists.
# No fix route: the registry now lists http-cache-semantics 4.3.0 (2026-10-04), but a diff of
# its index.js against 4.2.0 shows the max-stale handling unchanged (4.3.0 fixes the unrelated
# Vary-header issue, CVE-2026-93750), and Trivy still reports this id as `affected` with no fixed
# version. Even a fixed release could not be applied here: the copy is vendored inside pnpm's own
# dist bundle, so neither `pnpm.overrides` nor a lockfile change reaches it, and pnpm 10.34.6 is
# the newest 10.x release (D-05, Phase 102: stay on the 10.x line).
# Not reachable, measured in clokr-api:cve-test: the only dependency chain inside the pnpm
# bundle is node-gyp@11.5.0 -> make-fetch-happen@14.0.3 -> http-cache-semantics — node-gyp's
# download path for native addon builds during `pnpm install`. pnpm.cjs and worker.js contain
# zero occurrences of `http-cache-semantics` or `make-fetch-happen`. The runtime never installs:
# docker-entrypoint.sh has zero pnpm invocations and the only runtime pnpm call is
# `pnpm tsx scripts/anonymize-dump.ts` (charts/clokr-app/templates/cronjob-anonymizer.yaml:51),
# a run command against our own baked workspace. Verified dynamically, not just asserted: a Node
# `Module._resolveFilename` hook (`NODE_OPTIONS=--require <hook>.cjs`) attached to a real
# `pnpm tsx --version` in /app/apps/api (the CronJob's command shape and directory) logged 837
# real module-resolution calls; zero of them referenced `http-cache-semantics` or
# `make-fetch-happen`. The one line mentioning `node-gyp` is pnpm resolving the node-gyp bin PATH
# into a constant at start-up (pnpm.cjs:98349, resolveFrom(..., "node-gyp/bin/node-gyp")); no file
# under node-gyp/ was loaded, so its dependency closure was never entered.
# Corroborating only: make-fetch-happen's cache is a private on-disk download cache for the
# invoking process, not a shared multi-user cache answering other users' requests, which is the
# precondition of this CVE.
# Revisit: remove when http-cache-semantics publishes a release that fixes the max-stale issue
# AND a pnpm 10.x release vendors it (check the registry's semver-sorted 10.x tail, not just the
# latest tag), or when the monorepo moves to a pnpm line that vendors a fixed copy, or when pnpm
# leaves the API runtime image. Re-verify immediately if `http-cache-semantics` ever appears in
# pnpm-lock.yaml: an id-level ignore would then also cover an application copy.
CVE-2026-93748
